                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The archetypical example of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanical+system"} mechanical system
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            propagating on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of this particular system happens to be canonically identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ≔
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \coloneqq T^* \Sigma
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covector"} covector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x,p)
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            over a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \Sigma
            is physically interpretd as describing a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            of the system where the particle is at position
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in \Sigma
            and has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momentum"} momentum
            (essentially: speed) as given by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            Therefore locally for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+patch"} coordinate patch
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : \mathbb{R}^{2n} \simeq \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^n \to T^* \Sigma
            the
            = succeed "-canonical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            are naturally thought of as decomposed into
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            “canonical coordinates” on the first
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            factors and a set of “canonical momenta”, being the canonical coordinates on the second
            = succeed "-factor." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
          %p
            Notice that “canonical” here refers (at best) to the canonical coordinates of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                once
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ϕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                has been chosen
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            however is arbitrary. Hence, despite the (standard) term, there is nothing much canonical about these “canonical coordinates” and “canonical momenta”.
          %p
            In general, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physical+system"} physical system
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            which need not be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
            as for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
          %p
            But
            %em locally
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+patch"} coordinate patch
            every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            looks like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                  %mo ≃
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{2n} \simeq \mathbb{R}^n \times \mathbb{R}^n
            such that under this identification the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            reads
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %mi n
                    %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi d
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{i = 1}^n d q_i \wedge d p^i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{q_i\}
            the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinates"} coordinates
            on one
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{p^i\}
            for the other.
          %p
            Therefore generally, in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mechanics"} mechanics
            %em
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^i
            the
            %strong canonical momentum
            of the coordinate (or sometimes “canonical coordinate”)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi q
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q_i
          %p
            Globally the notion of canonical momenta may not exist at all. The notion that does exist globally is that of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarization"} polarization
            of a symplectic manifold. See there for more details.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#on_a_symplectic_vector_space On a symplectic vector space
          %p
            Discussion of how there is a flat connection on the bundle of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces+of+quantum+states"} spaces of quantum states
            over the space of choices of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarizations"} polarizations
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+vector+space"} symplectic vector space
            and how this reproduces the traditional relation between canonical coordinates and canonical momenta by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform"} Fourier transform
            is in (
            %a{:href => "#KirwinWu04"}> Kirwin-Wu 04
            ).
